-- title:
--   Compression None None
-- statement:
--   **The original effect is the none-to-none block of its Naimark projector.**
--
--   Let `data` be one-measurement Naimark dilation data for a finite-outcome submeasurement, let $a$ be an original outcome, and let $i,j$ be indices of the original Hilbert space. Write $L_{\mathrm{some}(a)}$ for the lifted projector associated with the auxiliary outcome `some a`, and write $M_a$ for the source effect. At the auxiliary `none` coordinates, the lifted projector has exactly the source effect as its matrix block:
--
--   $$
--   L_{\mathrm{some}(a)}\bigl((i,\bot),(j,\bot)\bigr)=M_a(i,j).
--   $$
--
--   Thus compressing the dilated outcome to the original input slice recovers each original matrix entry. This is the entrywise form of the one-measurement preservation identity used to connect the enlarged projective measurement with the source operators.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.MakingMeasurementsProjective.OneMeasNaimarkData.compression_none_none`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
